      "dom_id": "question-127374",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14194265/assets/88bec66b7ece961f2f0fc4083ce317f2bdc414e0a219d61bbf9bf73ae667ca0f.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the left-opening parabola shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "The diagram shows that the parabola has its vertex at $O,$ and passes through the point $(-2,2).$\n\nThe general equation of a left- or right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-2$ and $y=2$ into the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)^{2} & =4p(-2) \\\\ 4 & =-8p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=-2x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [],
      "problem_field_keys": [],
      "answer_fields_complete": null,
      "result": "Incorrect",
      "proof_feedback": null,
