      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14194265/assets/4c6a99f62a77f7fc16dbb95e8a8d03c42b266fffee10ed3739ab13820729be44.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the right-opening parabola shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "The diagram shows that the parabola has its vertex at $O,$ and passes through the point $(2,-3).$\n\nThe standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ and $y=-3$ into the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)^{2} & =4p(2) \\\\ 9 & =8p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=\\frac{9}{2}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{9}{2}x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{9}{4y}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
