    {
      "dom_id": "question-342215",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14194265/assets/c403f11093b76facafbecb72bcc413640ac67fe6ed73d6b64374230f39e29aba.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the left-opening parabola shown above. Express the answer in terms of the variable $y.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The diagram shows that the parabola has its vertex at $O,$ and passes through the point $(-3,-1).$\n\nThe general equation of a left- or right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-3$ and $y=-1$ into the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{2} & =4p(-3) \\\\ 1 & =-12p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{3}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{3}x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{6y}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
